{"kind":"request","text":"Claude: give the checkout read a deadline someone can see, and a failure that says so.\n\nWhy. internal/app/checkout_read.go:80 puts one fixed three-second bound over a\nwhole captured read — readRefInventory, landingRemote, and every Store.Lineage\ncall under it. Lineage walks up to gitstore.LineageCommitLimit, which is 512\ncommits, and re-verifies each commit object by hash. Three seconds is enough for\nthat when the disk is quiet and not enough when it is busy.\n\nThe project's own gate is when it is busiest. Under `go test ./...`,\ninternal/app competes with cmd/gs, internal/service and internal/kernel, and\nTestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated fails: two runs out of two\non one machine, while the same test passes five of five alone, and passes with\nthe whole package alone, and passes under eighteen busy loops on eighteen cores.\nIt is not CPU starvation — ulimit -n is 1048576, ulimit -u is 10666, and the disk\nhas 1.3 TiB free with inode use at zero percent. It is the wall clock running out\non work the machine is doing more slowly.\n\nNobody has been able to say that until now, because association.go:397 answers\nevery failure of that call with the fixed string \"commit lineage unavailable\".\nThe context deadline, the git exit status, and everything else arrive as the same\nseven words. A reader sees an association that is incomplete and no reason they\ncan act on, and the test that fails says exactly what a real defect would say.\n\nThis is the defect this repository has just spent a lane fixing in the CLI: a\nfixed short deadline on an operation that has to succeed, and a failure worded so\nthe deadline cannot be seen. The gate flakes because of it, and every lane that\nruns the gate pays.\n\nOutcome. A checkout read that runs out of time says so, in the projection and in\nthe test output, with what it was doing when it did. The bound is no longer a\nnumber written once in the source with no way to raise it for a machine that is\nslower or busier. And the gate stops failing for a reason nobody can read.","body":{"branch":"request/checkout-read-deadline","conditions":"1. A failed checkout read says why it failed. The reason reaching the projection and the test output distinguishes at least a deadline that expired, a git invocation that refused, and a limit the read was bounded by; \"commit lineage unavailable\" is no longer the answer to all of them. 2. The three-second bound in captureAround is no longer a literal that only a source edit can change: it takes its value the way the submit deadline does, defaulting to what it is today so an unconfigured run behaves as it does now. 3. A test pins the distinction: a read whose deadline expires reports a deadline, and a read that fails for another reason reports that reason, and neither is reported as the other. 4. TestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated and TestAnIncompleteAssociationWithholdsTheCleanupAdviceToo pass under whole-repository concurrency, demonstrated by running go test ./... at the head, and the report states how many times it was run and what each run did. 5. Mutation proofs for each new guard, run through the harness once it exists, or by hand with the three outcomes stated. 6. go vet ./... clean and go test ./... green at the exact head; independent review; ratified; landed and pushed.","target_head":"762f34ae6e5cc69ac3724aec3231edca03520479","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}